g its descent down  the fallopian tubes to enter the uterus  in hopes of meeting a nice little sperm  to settle down with this phase is met  with an increase in estrogen  testosterone follicle stimulating  hormone and luteinizing hormone which  explains why we feel more energized at  this point so take advantage of this  increase in libido sexual energy and  mood now is your time to work tip one  move in a way that feels good because  your energy levels are now a lot more  balanced in this phase you're probably  feeling more excited to hit the gym so  hit up some Pilates weight training spin  class or what I guess evolution  would want us to be doing of course  having sex our higher energy levels and  brighter mood are a good match for these  types of moderate to high intensity  activities so if you are an active  person and this brings you joy now is  the time for it tip number two use this  energy to prep again because this phase  allows for optimal energy levels this is  the best time to get in the kitchen and  cook up a storm  so get at your slow-cooker instant pot  and every casserole dish you own this is  when you want to do your batch prepping  for the week so chopping up vegetables  cooking proteins making fries soups and  casseroles for the week go get'em girl  now let's talk about the third and final  phase of the menstrual cycle the luteal  phase the luteal phase happens when  ovulation phase does not result in  fertilization aka  there ain't no bun in the oven when this  happens hormone levels decline and we  experience the oh so lovely PMS this may  include symptoms like cramps headaches  bloating mood swings fatigue and food  cravings which brings me to tip number  one notice your food cravings now we  have all been there you know eating  chocolate chip cookies and crying over a  cheesy rom-com for no real reason that  my friends is the luteal phase aka when  PMS hits but food cravings symptoms are  actually scientifically proven research  has found our food cravings during the  luteal phase are linked to the amount of  leptin in our blood leptin is the hunger  hormone that tells us when we're full  and we can stop eating studies have  actually shown that there may be a  relationship between leptin and estrogen  hormones and the food cravings that we  experience when leptin and estrogen  levels are high during the luteal phase  women tend to experience increased  cravings particularly for sweet carb  rich foods however when leptin and  estrogen levels are low women may  experience fewer cravings now we may not  be able to change our leptin or estrogen  levels but what we can do is listen to  our bodies and notice our cravings and  any patterns that arise during this  phase of menstruation  do you crave sweet foods or salty foods  are there certain textures that you  prefer maybe a satisfying crunch from a  bag of chips or the soft dough eNOS from  a doughnut I mean it's really important  we start to become more aware and  mindful of these food cravings in order  to assess what it is that your body  actually needs and that brings me to tip  number two let yourself have the carbs  as mentioned research suggests that we  tend to crave refined carbs during this  phase thanks to PMS and trust me I am  all for enjoying that cookie or bowl of  chocolate ice cream but this is a  long-ass phase of our cycle and when  we're about to lead ourselves into an  even more sluggish phase aka  our period right after I figure it's  ideal to have some really good fuel on  board also restricting all the carbs  right when you're craving them the most  is just going to kick off a massive  cookie binge and that is just not gonna  leave us feeling too hot either now is  the time to focus on slow burning but  delicious carbs maybe make a big batch  of overnight oats for breakfast pick up  a hearty CD bread for lunch or roast  some sweet potatoes with a little bit of  maple syrup and cinnamon for dinner and  when in doubt pair that car with a  source of protein and fat to keep those  blood sugar levels even keeled tip  number three up the fiber on a similar  note let's talk about fiber progesterone  levels are at an all-time high during  this phase right before your period and  we know that progesterone is one of the  major culprits of constipation so you're  gonna want to sneak in lots of fiber  rich whole grains like quinoa or bran  cereal things like berries or vegetables  like broccoli or kale flax beans and  nuts that brings me to tip number four  ease the bloat during the luteal phase  of menstruation and leading it to those  first few days of our period it's very  common feel bloated regardless of how  much or what you eat thanks to our  higher levels of estrogen and those  estrogen levels cause us to retain more  water therefore as I mentioned earlier  with the menstrual phase you'll want to  focus on more fresh foods instead of  highly processed salty foods you might  also want to  to plain water as carbonated drinks can  cause more gas and discomfort plus going  easy on foods that tend to cause you  digestive distress this obviously varies  from person to person but common  triggers are - mat foods like dairy  apples wheat and beans gassy veggies  like broccoli and kale sugar alcohols  and booze if you do have a higher sodium  meal and are feeling a little bit  uncomfortable don't sweat it  I mean you can pair it with foods that  are rich in water like cucumber lettuce  and citrus or potassium rich foods like  bananas and sweet potatoes to help  balance out the salt bottom-line us  ladies deal with our monthly period  every month so we are totally experts  right now in fact you're probably doing  a lot of these things without even  thinking about them because they  intuitively just feel good but if you're  finding some of your symptoms at  different times of the month totally  throwing you off I hope this video gives  you some evidence-based ideas on how you  can feel your best no matter what your  hormones throughout you well folks that  is all for today as always if you like  this video be sure to give it a thumbs  up leave me a comment below if you have  any fertility reproductive or hormonal  health questions subscribe to the  channel and I'll see you next time on  Abby's kitchen bye  [Music]

